<!--
    The themed-icon layer. Android throws the colours away and tints whatever alpha it finds, so
    this is the same mark in one ink rather than two.

    It is a separate file from the foreground even though the geometry is identical, because the two
    have different jobs the moment either changes: the foreground is free to reach for a second
    colour, a gradient or a knockout, and every one of those is meaningless here. Pointing the
    monochrome slot at the foreground — which is what this icon used to do — works only for as long
    as the foreground stays a flat silhouette, and stops working silently rather than loudly.

    Drawn a shade heavier than the foreground's 1.55. A tinted layer lands on whatever the user's
    wallpaper made the system pick, frequently a mid-tone against a mid-tone, and the mark loses the
    contrast the ice-on-night plate was giving it for free.
-->
